On Wed, Oct 15, 2014 at 7:25 PM, Ian Johnson <blindbravado@gmail.com> wrote:

> My mental algorithm is a somewhat more unified version of yours. I look at
> it as CMAVO-X. If X would be a gismu form or a lujvo form, then CMAVO-X is
> CMAVO X. Otherwise it stays together. The CMAVO in CVC-X is the CV; the
> CMAVO in CV'V-X is the CV'V; the CMAVO in CVV-X is the CVV. We fix the
> first case with a {y} hyphen; we fix the other two cases with a consonant
> hyphen ({r}, {n}, {l}, in that order).
>

Yes. athough for the r-hyphen case the condition "if X would be a gismu
form or a lujvo form" is not enough, it could also be one or more cmavo
forms followed by a gismu form, a lujvo form, or nothing: e,g,  CVV+CVV
requires -r-, CVV+CVV+CCVCV requires -r-, and so on.

One nice thing about this view is that it shows how "tosmabru" and
> "slinku'i" are dual to one another. "slinku'i" is the manifestation of the
> rule that when X is a zi'evla (fu'ivla in CLL terminology), CMAVO-X does
> *not* fragment.
>

I think you mean "when X is a slinku'i". A slinku'i is not a valid zi'evla

mu'o mi'e xorxes

